Kubernetes YAML Files — Simplified for DevOps Enthusiasts! 🧵


Here’s a quick breakdown of some must-know Kubernetes manifest files:

🧱 pod.yaml – Defines one or more containers to run with shared resources.
⚙️ deployment.yaml – Manages automated updates and scaling of pods.
🔗 service.yaml – Connects internal/external services through stable endpoints.
🔧 configmap.yaml – Keeps configuration data outside of application code.
🔒 secret.yaml – Stores sensitive data securely (API keys, passwords).
🌐 ingress.yaml – Controls external access to your services.
💾 pvc.yaml – Requests persistent storage for stateful apps.
📈 hpa.yaml – Scales pods based on CPU/memory usage.
🧑🔧 serviceaccount.yaml – Allows pods to interact with Kubernetes APIs securely.
🛡️ role.yaml – Sets fine-grained access controls within namespaces.
🔄 rolebinding.yaml – Binds roles to users or service accounts.
🏢 clusterrole.yaml – Grants cluster-wide permissions.
🧩 daemonset.yaml – Ensures a pod runs on all (or specific) nodes.
⏱️ job.yaml – Executes a task once and stops when complete.
🚦 networkpolicy.yaml – Controls how pods talk to each other.
These files are the backbone of Infrastructure as Code (IaC) in Kubernetes!
